{"id":10,"title":"Laws of Exponents & Roots — Fundamentals","subject":"algebra","sections":[{"id":"power-theory","title":"Eight guided questions","kind":"learn","steps":[{"kind":"video","id":"powers-techniques","minutes":2.5,"title":"Exponents & Roots — Techniques"},{"kind":"question","id":"q-248","stem":"\\(\\frac{4^{5}\\cdot27^{-2}}{2^{7}\\cdot9^{-2}}=?\\)","choices":["\\(2^{-2}\\cdot3^{3}\\)","\\(2^{3}\\cdot3^{-2}\\)","\\(\\frac{3^{2}}{2^{3}}\\)","\\(\\frac{2^{3}}{3^{10}}\\)"],"correct":1,"explanation":["Write every base as a prime: \\(4^5=(2^2)^5=2^{10}\\), \\(27^{-2}=(3^3)^{-2}=3^{-6}\\), \\(9^{-2}=(3^2)^{-2}=3^{-4}\\).","The fraction becomes \\(\\frac{2^{10}\\cdot3^{-6}}{2^7\\cdot3^{-4}}=2^{10-7}\\cdot3^{-6-(-4)}=2^3\\cdot3^{-2}\\).","The trap is choice 4: \\(-6-(-4)\\) is \\(-2\\), not \\(-10\\) (minus minus is plus)."],"number":1},{"kind":"video","id":"solve-q-248","minutes":1.6,"solution":true,"questionId":"q-248","title":"Worked solution · Question 1"},{"kind":"question","id":"q-249","stem":"\\(\\frac{\\sqrt{72}+\\sqrt{72}}{\\sqrt{98}-\\sqrt{32}}=?\\)","choices":["\\(4\\sqrt2\\)","\\(2\\)","\\(16\\)","\\(4\\)"],"correct":3,"explanation":["Simplify each root: \\(\\sqrt{72}=6\\sqrt2\\), \\(\\sqrt{98}=7\\sqrt2\\), \\(\\sqrt{32}=4\\sqrt2\\).","Numerator: \\(6\\sqrt2+6\\sqrt2=12\\sqrt2\\). Denominator: \\(7\\sqrt2-4\\sqrt2=3\\sqrt2\\).","\\(\\frac{12\\sqrt2}{3\\sqrt2}=4\\).","Or with a common factor: the top is \\(2\\sqrt{72}\\), the bottom is \\(\\sqrt2(\\sqrt{49}-\\sqrt{16})=3\\sqrt2\\), and \\(\\frac{2\\sqrt{72}}{3\\sqrt2}=\\frac23\\sqrt{36}=\\frac23\\cdot6=4\\)."],"number":2},{"kind":"video","id":"solve-q-249","minutes":2.1,"solution":true,"questionId":"q-249","title":"Worked solution · Question 2"},{"kind":"question","id":"q-250","stem":"Given: \\(9^{2x}=\\left(\\frac{1}{27}\\right)^{2-2x}\\)\n\\(x=?\\)","choices":["\\(1\\)","\\(\\frac35\\)","\\(3\\)","\\(-3\\)"],"correct":2,"explanation":["Write both sides with base 3: \\(9^{2x}=(3^2)^{2x}=3^{4x}\\), and \\(\\frac1{27}=3^{-3}\\), so \\(\\left(\\frac1{27}\\right)^{2-2x}=3^{-3(2-2x)}=3^{-6+6x}\\).","Equal bases, so the exponents are equal: \\(4x=-6+6x\\). Therefore \\(2x=6\\) and \\(x=3\\).","Check: \\(9^6=3^{12}\\) and \\(\\left(\\frac1{27}\\right)^{-4}=27^4=3^{12}\\) ✓. The trap \\(\\frac35\\) forgets the minus of the fraction."],"number":3},{"kind":"video","id":"solve-q-250","minutes":1.6,"solution":true,"questionId":"q-250","title":"Worked solution · Question 3"},{"kind":"question","id":"q-251","stem":"Given: \\(\\sqrt{x-6}=5\\)\n\\(x=?\\)","choices":["\\(11\\)","\\(19\\)","\\(31\\)","\\(25\\)"],"correct":2,"explanation":["Square both sides: \\(x-6=25\\), so \\(x=31\\).","Check: \\(\\sqrt{31-6}=\\sqrt{25}=5\\) ✓.","The traps: \\(11\\) forgets to square the \\(5\\), and \\(25\\) forgets to move the \\(6\\)."],"number":4},{"kind":"video","id":"solve-q-251","minutes":0.4,"solution":true,"questionId":"q-251","title":"Worked solution · Question 4"},{"kind":"question","id":"q-252","stem":"Given:\n\\(\\begin{cases} x\\ne0 \\\\ x\\sqrt6=6\\sqrt x \\end{cases}\\)\n\\(x=?\\)","choices":["\\(1\\)","\\(\\sqrt6\\)","\\(36\\)","\\(6\\)"],"correct":3,"explanation":["Square both sides: \\((x\\sqrt6)^2=(6\\sqrt x)^2\\), so \\(6x^2=36x\\).","Divide by 6: \\(x^2=6x\\). Since \\(x\\ne0\\), divide by \\(x\\): \\(x=6\\).","Check: \\(6\\sqrt6=6\\sqrt6\\) ✓.","If \\(x\\ne0\\) were not given, factor instead of dividing: \\(x(x-6)=0\\), so \\(x=0\\) or \\(x=6\\)."],"number":5},{"kind":"video","id":"solve-q-252","minutes":1.1,"solution":true,"questionId":"q-252","title":"Worked solution · Question 5"},{"kind":"question","id":"q-r26-t10-01","stem":"Given: \\(\\sqrt{2x+3}=x\\)\n\\(x=?\\)","choices":["\\(1\\)","\\(-1\\)","\\(3\\)","\\(-1\\) or \\(3\\)"],"correct":2,"explanation":["A root is never negative, and here \\(x\\) equals a root, so \\(x\\ge0\\). Choices 2 (\\(-1\\)) and 4 (\\(-1\\) or \\(3\\)) are out at once.","Try the other two: \\(x=1\\): \\(\\sqrt{2+3}=\\sqrt5\\ne1\\) ✗. \\(x=3\\): \\(\\sqrt{6+3}=\\sqrt9=3\\) ✓. So \\(x=3\\).","Another way: square both sides: \\(2x+3=x^2\\). No need to solve it — check the candidates from the choices in the ORIGINAL equation.","\\(x=-1\\) satisfies the squared equation (\\(2\\cdot(-1)+3=1=(-1)^2\\)) but not the original (\\(\\sqrt1=1\\ne-1\\)): a fake solution created by squaring. After squaring, always check in the original.","Choice 4 (\\(-1\\) or \\(3\\)) is the trap: it is what you get if you square and forget to check."],"number":6},{"kind":"video","id":"solve-q-r26-t10-01","minutes":1.5,"solution":true,"questionId":"q-r26-t10-01","title":"Worked solution · Question 6"},{"kind":"video","id":"r26-t10-power-traps","minutes":0.9,"title":"Exponent Traps — Sums of Powers"},{"kind":"question","id":"q-r26-t10-02","stem":"\\(2^{10}+2^{10}+2^{10}+2^{10}=?\\)","choices":["\\(8^{10}\\)","\\(2^{12}\\)","\\(2^{40}\\)","\\(8^{40}\\)"],"correct":1,"explanation":["Four equal powers: \\(2^{10}+2^{10}+2^{10}+2^{10}=4\\cdot2^{10}\\).","\\(4=2^2\\), so \\(4\\cdot2^{10}=2^2\\cdot2^{10}=2^{12}\\).","Adding the exponents (\\(2^{40}\\)) or adding the bases (\\(8^{10}\\)) is the trap: there is no law for adding powers."],"number":7},{"kind":"video","id":"solve-q-r26-t10-02","minutes":0.9,"solution":true,"questionId":"q-r26-t10-02","title":"Worked solution · Question 7"},{"kind":"question","id":"q-r26-t10-03","stem":"Given: \\(3^{x+2}-3^x=72\\)\n\\(x=?\\)","choices":["\\(1\\)","\\(2\\)","\\(3\\)","\\(4\\)"],"correct":1,"explanation":["Take out the smallest power: \\(3^{x+2}-3^x=3^x(3^2-1)=8\\cdot3^x\\).","\\(8\\cdot3^x=72\\), so \\(3^x=9=3^2\\) and \\(x=2\\).","Check: \\(3^4-3^2=81-9=72\\) ✓."],"number":8},{"kind":"video","id":"solve-q-r26-t10-03","minutes":0.9,"solution":true,"questionId":"q-r26-t10-03","title":"Worked solution · Question 8"},{"kind":"card","id":"mem-r26-t10-techniques","title":"Exponent and root techniques","intro":"The methods of this topic, each with one example.","tables":[{"title":"Techniques","head":["Situation","Method","Example"],"rows":[["Number over a root","ignore the root, then put it back","\\(\\frac{10}{\\sqrt2}=5\\sqrt2\\)"],["Different bases","write every base as the smallest prime","\\(8^4=2^{12}=16^3\\)"],["Negative exponent","flip the base","\\(7^2\\cdot2^{-5}=\\frac{7^2}{2^5}\\)"],["Adding roots","split, or take a common factor","\\(\\sqrt{44}+\\sqrt{99}=5\\sqrt{11}\\)"],["Equal powers added","count the copies","\\(2^x+2^x=2\\cdot2^x=2^{x+1}\\)"],["Same base, different exponents","take out the smallest power","\\(2^{x+2}-2^x=3\\cdot2^x\\)"],["Same exponent","multiply the bases","\\(2^x\\cdot5^x=10^x\\)"],["Power equation","equal bases, then equal exponents","\\(3^{2x-1}=3^3\\Rightarrow x=2\\)"],["Root equation","square, solve, check in the original","\\(\\sqrt{x+2}=x\\Rightarrow x=2\\) only"]]}],"tips":["Trap: \\(2^x+2^x\\ne4^x\\). Check with \\(x=3\\): \\(8+8=16\\), but \\(4^3=64\\).","Mixed bases in a fraction? Pair the families: \\(\\frac{4^5\\cdot27^{-2}}{2^7\\cdot9^{-2}}=\\frac{4^5}{2^7}\\cdot\\left(\\frac{27}{9}\\right)^{-2}\\).","Four choices? Try them. It is often faster, and it throws out fake solutions of root equations.","If \\(x\\ne0\\) is NOT given, factor — don't divide by \\(x\\): \\(x^2=6x\\Rightarrow x(x-6)=0\\).","Expression choices? Choose an easy value (\\(x=2\\) or \\(x=3\\)). If two choices match, try another number."]},{"kind":"video","id":"r26-t10-summary","minutes":2.6,"title":"Exponents & Roots — Summary"}]},{"id":"unit-t10-2","title":"Expressions and equations","kind":"practice","steps":[{"kind":"question","id":"q-254","stem":"\\(2^3\\cdot4^4=?\\)","choices":["\\(2^{7}\\)","\\(2^{9}\\)","\\(2^{11}\\)","\\(2^{12}\\)"],"correct":2,"explanation":["\\(4^4=(2^2)^4=2^8\\), so \\(2^3\\cdot2^8=2^{3+8}=2^{11}\\)."]},{"kind":"question","id":"q-256","stem":"Given: \\(5^3=5^{x+8}\\)\n\\(x=?\\)","choices":["\\(-5\\)","\\(5\\)","\\(-11\\)","\\(11\\)"],"correct":0,"explanation":["Equal bases, so the exponents are equal: \\(x+8=3\\). Therefore \\(x=-5\\)."]},{"kind":"question","id":"q-262","stem":"\\(\\sqrt{28} = ?\\)","choices":["\\(7\\,\\sqrt{2}\\)","\\(2\\,\\sqrt{7}\\)","\\(4\\,\\sqrt{7}\\)","\\(2\\,\\sqrt{14}\\)"],"correct":1,"explanation":["Pull out the largest square: \\(28=4\\cdot7\\), so \\(\\sqrt{28}=\\sqrt4\\cdot\\sqrt7=2\\sqrt7\\)."]},{"kind":"question","id":"q-263","stem":"\\(\\sqrt{117}=?\\)","choices":["\\(13\\sqrt3\\)","\\(4\\sqrt3\\)","\\(9\\sqrt{13}\\)","\\(3\\sqrt{13}\\)"],"correct":3,"explanation":["\\(117=9\\cdot13\\), therefore \\(\\sqrt{117}=\\sqrt9\\cdot\\sqrt{13}=3\\sqrt{13}\\)."]},{"kind":"question","id":"q-264","stem":"\\(\\sqrt2+\\sqrt{32}=?\\)","choices":["\\(3\\sqrt2\\)","\\(4\\sqrt2\\)","\\(5\\sqrt2\\)","\\(6\\sqrt2\\)"],"correct":2,"explanation":["\\(\\sqrt{32}=\\sqrt{16}\\cdot\\sqrt2=4\\sqrt2\\).","\\(\\sqrt2+4\\sqrt2=5\\sqrt2\\)."]},{"kind":"question","id":"q-265","stem":"\\(\\sqrt{75}-\\sqrt{12}=?\\)","choices":["\\(7\\sqrt3\\)","\\(\\sqrt{63}\\)","\\(2\\sqrt3\\)","\\(3\\sqrt3\\)"],"correct":3,"explanation":["\\(\\sqrt{75}=\\sqrt{25}\\cdot\\sqrt3=5\\sqrt3\\) and \\(\\sqrt{12}=\\sqrt4\\cdot\\sqrt3=2\\sqrt3\\).","\\(5\\sqrt3-2\\sqrt3=3\\sqrt3\\).","The trap \\(\\sqrt{63}\\) subtracts under the root, but \\(\\sqrt{75}-\\sqrt{12}\\ne\\sqrt{75-12}\\)."]},{"kind":"question","id":"q-260","stem":"\\(\\frac{21}{\\sqrt3}=?\\)","choices":["\\(3\\sqrt7\\)","\\(7\\sqrt3\\)","\\(7\\sqrt7\\)","\\(3\\sqrt3\\)"],"correct":1,"explanation":["Ignore the root: \\(21\\div3=7\\). Put the root back on the answer: \\(7\\sqrt3\\).","Why it works: \\(21=7\\cdot\\sqrt3\\cdot\\sqrt3\\), so \\(\\frac{7\\cdot\\sqrt3\\cdot\\sqrt3}{\\sqrt3}=7\\sqrt3\\)."]},{"kind":"question","id":"q-261","stem":"\\(\\frac{7\\sqrt2}{\\sqrt{14}}=?\\)","choices":["\\(1\\)","\\(\\sqrt2\\)","\\(7\\)","\\(\\sqrt7\\)"],"correct":3,"explanation":["Split the denominator: \\(\\sqrt{14}=\\sqrt2\\cdot\\sqrt7\\).","Cancel \\(\\sqrt2\\): \\(\\frac{7\\sqrt2}{\\sqrt2\\cdot\\sqrt7}=\\frac7{\\sqrt7}\\).","Number over a root: \\(7\\div7=1\\), then put the root back: \\(\\sqrt7\\)."]},{"kind":"question","id":"q-266","stem":"Given: \\(\\sqrt{3x+1}=4\\)\n\\(x=?\\)","choices":["\\(1\\)","\\(\\frac{17}{3}\\)","\\(3\\)","\\(5\\)"],"correct":3,"explanation":["Square both sides: \\(3x+1=16\\), so \\(3x=15\\) and \\(x=5\\).","Check: \\(\\sqrt{3\\cdot5+1}=\\sqrt{16}=4\\) ✓."]},{"kind":"question","id":"q-267","stem":"Given: \\(\\sqrt{5x-4}=\\sqrt{66}\\)\n\\(x=?\\)","choices":["\\(14\\)","\\(12\\)","\\(15\\)","\\(13\\)"],"correct":0,"explanation":["Square both sides: \\(5x-4=66\\), so \\(5x=70\\) and \\(x=14\\)."]},{"kind":"question","id":"q-257","stem":"Given: \\(3^{10}=9^{x-2}\\)\n\\(x=?\\)","choices":["\\(12\\)","\\(7\\)","\\(6\\)","\\(5\\)"],"correct":1,"explanation":["\\(9=3^2\\), so \\(9^{x-2}=3^{2(x-2)}=3^{2x-4}\\).","Equal exponents: \\(2x-4=10\\), so \\(2x=14\\) and \\(x=7\\)."]},{"kind":"question","id":"q-258","stem":"Given: \\(27^4=9^3\\cdot3^x\\)\n\\(x=?\\)","choices":["\\(1\\)","\\(6\\)","\\(3\\)","\\(12\\)"],"correct":1,"explanation":["Base 3 everywhere: \\(27^4=3^{12}\\) and \\(9^3=3^6\\).","\\(3^{12}=3^6\\cdot3^x=3^{6+x}\\), so \\(6+x=12\\) and \\(x=6\\)."]},{"kind":"question","id":"q-259","stem":"Given: \\(\\left(\\frac13\\right)^4=3^{x-6}\\)\n\\(x=?\\)","choices":["\\(10\\)","\\(2\\)","\\(-2\\)","\\(-10\\)"],"correct":1,"explanation":["\\(\\frac13=3^{-1}\\), so \\(\\left(\\frac13\\right)^4=3^{-4}\\).","Equal exponents: \\(x-6=-4\\), so \\(x=2\\)."]},{"kind":"question","id":"q-253","stem":"\\(\\frac{2^7}{6^2}=?\\)","choices":["\\(\\frac{32}{3}\\)","\\(\\frac{16}{9}\\)","\\(\\frac{16}{3}\\)","\\(\\frac{32}{9}\\)"],"correct":3,"explanation":["\\(6^2=(2\\cdot3)^2=2^2\\cdot3^2\\).","\\(\\frac{2^7}{2^2\\cdot3^2}=\\frac{2^5}{3^2}=\\frac{32}{9}\\)."]},{"kind":"question","id":"q-255","stem":"\\(\\frac{9^4\\cdot27^2}{81\\cdot3^6}=?\\)","choices":["\\(3^{4}\\)","\\(3^{5}\\)","\\(3^{3}\\)","\\(3^{6}\\)"],"correct":0,"explanation":["All bases are powers of 3: \\(9^4=3^8\\), \\(27^2=3^6\\), \\(81=3^4\\).","Numerator: \\(3^8\\cdot3^6=3^{14}\\). Denominator: \\(3^4\\cdot3^6=3^{10}\\).","\\(\\frac{3^{14}}{3^{10}}=3^{14-10}=3^4\\)."]},{"kind":"question","id":"q-r26-t10-06","stem":"\\(5^x+5^x+5^x+5^x+5^x=?\\)","choices":["\\(25^x\\)","\\(5^{5x}\\)","\\(5^{x+1}\\)","\\(25^{5x}\\)"],"correct":2,"explanation":["Five equal powers: \\(5^x+5^x+5^x+5^x+5^x=5\\cdot5^x=5^1\\cdot5^x=5^{x+1}\\).","If you check with a number, avoid \\(x=1\\) (then \\(25^x=25\\) too). With \\(x=2\\): \\(5\\cdot25=125=5^3\\), while \\(25^2=625\\)."]}]},{"id":"unit-t10-3","title":"Mixed practice","kind":"practice","steps":[{"kind":"question","id":"q-276","stem":"\\(6^x\\cdot6^{-x}=?\\)","choices":["\\(1\\)","\\(36\\)","\\(\\frac16\\)","\\(6\\)"],"correct":0,"explanation":["Add the exponents: \\(x+(-x)=0\\), so the product is \\(6^0=1\\)."]},{"kind":"question","id":"q-277","stem":"Given: \\(m=n=6\\)\n\\(m^{n-m}\\cdot n^{m-n}=?\\)","choices":["\\(1\\)","\\(36\\)","\\(0\\)","\\(6\\)"],"correct":0,"explanation":["Since \\(m=n=6\\), both exponents are zero: \\(n-m=6-6=0\\) and \\(m-n=6-6=0\\).","Therefore the product is \\(6^0\\cdot6^0=1\\cdot1=1\\)."]},{"kind":"question","id":"q-283","stem":"\\(\\sqrt{112}=?\\)","choices":["\\(16\\sqrt7\\)","\\(7\\sqrt2\\)","\\(2\\sqrt{14}\\)","\\(4\\sqrt7\\)"],"correct":3,"explanation":["\\(112=16\\cdot7\\), therefore \\(\\sqrt{112}=\\sqrt{16}\\cdot\\sqrt7=4\\sqrt7\\)."]},{"kind":"question","id":"q-274","stem":"Given: \\(3^{n+2}=243\\)\n\\(n=?\\)","choices":["\\(1\\)","\\(5\\)","\\(3\\)","\\(7\\)"],"correct":2,"explanation":["\\(243=3^5\\), so \\(n+2=5\\) and \\(n=3\\)."]},{"kind":"question","id":"q-268","stem":"Given: \\(y>0\\)\n\\(y^{y+4}\\cdot y^{-y-5}=?\\)","choices":["\\(1\\)","\\(\\frac1y\\)","\\(y\\)","\\(0\\)"],"correct":1,"explanation":["Same base: add the exponents. \\((y+4)+(-y-5)=-1\\), so the product is \\(y^{-1}=\\frac1y\\).","Faster: choose an easy value, \\(y=2\\): \\(2^6\\cdot2^{-7}=2^{-1}=\\frac12\\). Only choice (2) gives \\(\\frac12\\) (the others are \\(1\\), \\(2\\) and \\(0\\))."]},{"kind":"question","id":"q-270","stem":"Given: \\(b>0\\)\n\\(\\frac{b^{b+3}}{b^3}=?\\)","choices":["\\(b^b\\)","\\((b+3)^b\\)","\\(2b\\)","\\(b^{b+6}\\)"],"correct":0,"explanation":["Same base: subtract the exponents. \\(\\frac{b^{b+3}}{b^3}=b^{b+3-3}=b^b\\).","With easy values: \\(b=2\\) gives \\(\\frac{2^5}{2^3}=4\\), but two choices give \\(4\\) (\\(b^b\\) and \\(2b\\)). Try another number: \\(b=3\\) gives \\(\\frac{3^6}{3^3}=27\\), and only \\(b^b=27\\) fits."]},{"kind":"question","id":"q-278","stem":"Given: \\(x>0\\) and \\(y>0\\)\n\\(\\frac{3x^9y^4}{x^3y^2}=?\\)","choices":["\\(3x^6y^2\\)","\\(3x^3y^2\\)","\\(3x^{12}y^6\\)","\\(3x^9y^4\\)"],"correct":0,"explanation":["Handle each base on its own and subtract the exponents: \\(x^{9-3}=x^6\\) and \\(y^{4-2}=y^2\\).","The 3 does not change: \\(3x^6y^2\\).","Shortcut · Power count: the power of a piece is how many letters are multiplied in it (a number in front counts \\(0\\)), and dividing subtracts. Simplifying never changes the power, so the right answer has the question's power. The top \\(x^9y^4\\) has \\(9+4=13\\) letters multiplied and the bottom \\(x^3y^2\\) has \\(5\\), so the question has power \\(13-5=8\\).","The choices have powers \\(8\\), \\(5\\), \\(18\\) and \\(13\\). Only choice 1 has power \\(8\\)."]},{"kind":"question","id":"q-279","stem":"\\(\\left(2^3\\right)^4\\cdot2^{-15}=?\\)","choices":["\\(-8\\)","\\(\\frac{1}{256}\\)","\\(\\frac{1}{8}\\)","\\(-\\frac{1}{8}\\)"],"correct":2,"explanation":["Power of a power: multiply the exponents. \\((2^3)^4=2^{12}\\).","\\(2^{12}\\cdot2^{-15}=2^{-3}=\\frac1{2^3}=\\frac18\\).","The traps: adding \\(3+4\\) gives \\(2^{-8}=\\frac1{256}\\), and a negative exponent never makes the number negative."]},{"kind":"question","id":"q-272","stem":"\\(\\frac{8^3\\cdot2^5}{32}=?\\)","choices":["\\(2^{4}\\)","\\(2\\)","\\(2^{14}\\)","\\(2^{9}\\)"],"correct":3,"explanation":["Base 2 everywhere: \\(8^3=2^9\\) and \\(32=2^5\\).","\\(\\frac{2^9\\cdot2^5}{2^5}=2^9\\)."]},{"kind":"question","id":"q-271","stem":"\\(27^x\\cdot9^x\\cdot3^x=?\\)","choices":["\\(3^{4x}\\)","\\(3^{6x}\\)","\\(3^{3x}\\)","\\(27^{3x}\\)"],"correct":1,"explanation":["Write every base as a power of 3: \\(27^x=3^{3x}\\), \\(9^x=3^{2x}\\).","Add the exponents: \\(3x+2x+x=6x\\), so the product is \\(3^{6x}\\)."]},{"kind":"question","id":"q-273","stem":"\\(25^4=?\\)","choices":["\\(125^{2}\\)","\\(50^{2}\\)","\\(5^{8}\\)","\\(\\left(5^2\\right)^{8}\\)"],"correct":2,"explanation":["\\(25=5^2\\), so \\(25^4=(5^2)^4=5^8\\).","The others: \\(125^2=(5^3)^2=5^6\\), \\((5^2)^8=5^{16}\\), and \\(50^2=(2\\cdot25)^2=4\\cdot5^4\\). None of them is \\(5^8\\)."]},{"kind":"question","id":"q-269","stem":"Given: \\(\\sqrt{x^2}=7\\). Which of the following could be the value of \\(x\\)?","choices":["\\(49\\)","\\(\\frac17\\)","\\(-1\\)","\\(-7\\)"],"correct":3,"explanation":["\\(\\sqrt{x^2}=|x|\\), so \\(|x|=7\\): \\(x=7\\) or \\(x=-7\\).","Only \\(-7\\) is among the choices."]},{"kind":"question","id":"q-280","stem":"Given: \\(x>0\\)\n\\(x^{\\frac25}\\cdot x^{\\frac52}=?\\)","choices":["\\(1\\)","\\(x^{\\frac{29}{10}}\\)","\\(x^{\\frac{10}{29}}\\)","\\(x\\)"],"correct":1,"explanation":["Same base: add the exponents. \\(\\frac25+\\frac52=\\frac4{10}+\\frac{25}{10}=\\frac{29}{10}\\).","So the product is \\(x^{\\frac{29}{10}}\\). (Multiplying the exponents gives \\(x^1=x\\) — the trap.)"]},{"kind":"question","id":"q-275","stem":"\\(\\left(2^{-\\sqrt5}\\right)^{-\\sqrt5}=?\\)","choices":["\\(2^{\\sqrt5}\\)","\\(2\\)","\\(32\\)","\\(4^{\\sqrt5}\\)"],"correct":2,"explanation":["Power of a power: multiply the exponents. \\((-\\sqrt5)\\cdot(-\\sqrt5)=(\\sqrt5)^2=5\\).","So the expression is \\(2^5=32\\)."]},{"kind":"question","id":"q-282","stem":"Given: \\(a+b+c=4\\)\n\\(2^a\\cdot2^b\\cdot2^c=?\\)","choices":["\\(8\\)","\\(16\\)","\\(64\\)","It cannot be determined from the information given."],"correct":1,"explanation":["\\(2^a\\cdot2^b\\cdot2^c=2^{a+b+c}=2^4=16\\).","We do not need \\(a\\), \\(b\\) and \\(c\\) one by one — only their sum, and it is given."]},{"kind":"question","id":"q-285","stem":"\\(\\sqrt[4]{7^6}=?\\)","choices":["\\(7^2\\)","\\(7\\sqrt7\\)","\\(\\sqrt7\\)","\\(\\sqrt[4]7\\)"],"correct":1,"explanation":["\\(\\sqrt[4]{7^6}=7^{\\frac64}=7^{\\frac32}=7^1\\cdot7^{\\frac12}=7\\sqrt7\\)."]},{"kind":"question","id":"q-286","stem":"Given:\n\\(\\begin{cases} x>0 \\\\ \\sqrt{50x}=\\sqrt2\\cdot x \\end{cases}\\)\n\\(x=?\\)","choices":["\\(25\\)","\\(2\\)","\\(5\\)","\\(\\sqrt2\\)"],"correct":0,"explanation":["Square both sides: \\(50x=2x^2\\).","Since \\(x>0\\), divide by \\(2x\\): \\(25=x\\).","Check: \\(\\sqrt{50\\cdot25}=\\sqrt{625\\cdot2}=25\\sqrt2\\) and \\(\\sqrt2\\cdot25=25\\sqrt2\\) ✓."]},{"kind":"question","id":"q-281","stem":"Given: \\(2^x\\cdot3^x\\cdot7^x=\\sqrt{42}\\)\n\\(x=?\\)","choices":["\\(1\\)","\\(2\\)","\\(\\frac13\\)","\\(\\frac12\\)"],"correct":3,"explanation":["Same exponent, so multiply the bases: \\(2^x\\cdot3^x\\cdot7^x=(2\\cdot3\\cdot7)^x=42^x\\).","\\(\\sqrt{42}=42^{\\frac12}\\). Equal bases, so \\(x=\\frac12\\)."]},{"kind":"question","id":"q-r26-t10-11","stem":"\\(\\frac{7^{n+1}-7^n}{6}=?\\)","choices":["\\(7\\)","\\(7^n\\)","\\(7^{n-1}\\)","\\(\\frac76\\)"],"correct":1,"explanation":["Take out the smallest power: \\(7^{n+1}-7^n=7^n(7-1)=6\\cdot7^n\\).","\\(\\frac{6\\cdot7^n}{6}=7^n\\)."]},{"kind":"question","id":"q-r26-t10-10","stem":"Given: \\(x\\sqrt3=\\sqrt{3x}\\). Which of the following gives all the possible values of \\(x\\)?","choices":["\\(1\\) only","\\(0\\) only","\\(0\\) or \\(1\\)","\\(3\\) only"],"correct":2,"explanation":["Square both sides: \\(3x^2=3x\\), so \\(x^2=x\\).","\\(x\\ne0\\) is NOT given, so do not divide by \\(x\\). Factor: \\(x^2-x=0\\), \\(x(x-1)=0\\): \\(x=0\\) or \\(x=1\\).","Check: \\(x=0\\): \\(0=\\sqrt0\\) ✓. \\(x=1\\): \\(\\sqrt3=\\sqrt3\\) ✓. Both work."]},{"kind":"question","id":"q-r26-t10-12","stem":"Given: \\(2^x+2^x=4^x\\)\n\\(x=?\\)","choices":["\\(1\\)","\\(2\\)","\\(0\\)","Every number \\(x\\) satisfies the equation."],"correct":0,"explanation":["Left side: \\(2^x+2^x=2\\cdot2^x=2^{x+1}\\). Right side: \\(4^x=2^{2x}\\).","Equal exponents: \\(x+1=2x\\), so \\(x=1\\).","Check: \\(2+2=4=4^1\\) ✓. With \\(x=2\\): \\(4+4=8\\ne16\\), so choice (4) is the trap \\(2^x+2^x=4^x\\)."]},{"kind":"question","id":"q-r26-t10-16","stem":"Given: \\(3^{20}+3^{20}+3^{20}=9^n\\)\n\\(n=?\\)","choices":["\\(10\\)","\\(10.5\\)","\\(21\\)","\\(30\\)"],"correct":1,"explanation":["Left side: \\(3\\cdot3^{20}=3^{21}\\). Right side: \\(9^n=(3^2)^n=3^{2n}\\).","Equal exponents: \\(2n=21\\), so \\(n=10.5\\)."]},{"kind":"question","id":"q-284","stem":"The numbers \\(m\\) and \\(n\\) are such that \\(x^m\\cdot x^n=x^{mn}\\) for every \\(x>1\\). Which of the following is necessarily true?","choices":["\\(m+n=0\\)","\\(m^n=m+n\\)","\\(m\\cdot n=m+n\\)","None of the above is necessarily true."],"correct":2,"explanation":["Same base: \\(x^m\\cdot x^n=x^{m+n}\\) always.","This equals \\(x^{mn}\\) for every \\(x>1\\), so the exponents are equal: \\(m+n=mn\\).","Choices (1) and (2) are not necessarily true. For example, \\(m=3\\) and \\(n=\\frac32\\): \\(m+n=mn=4.5\\), but \\(m+n\\ne0\\), and \\(3^{1.5}=\\sqrt{3^3}=\\sqrt{27}>\\sqrt{25}=5\\), so \\(m^n\\ne4.5\\)."]},{"kind":"question","id":"q-287","stem":"Noa says: \\(8>\\sqrt7+\\sqrt{28}\\).\nEthan says: \\(3\\sqrt5>4\\sqrt3\\).\nWhich of the following is correct?","choices":["Both are right.","Only Ethan is right.","Only Noa is right.","Both are wrong."],"correct":2,"explanation":["Noa: \\(\\sqrt{28}=2\\sqrt7\\), so \\(\\sqrt7+\\sqrt{28}=3\\sqrt7\\). Square both: \\((3\\sqrt7)^2=63\\) and \\(8^2=64\\). Since \\(63<64\\), Noa is right.","Ethan: square both: \\((3\\sqrt5)^2=45\\) and \\((4\\sqrt3)^2=48\\). Since \\(45<48\\), \\(3\\sqrt5<4\\sqrt3\\) and Ethan is wrong.","Only Noa is right."]}]}],"passages":{}}